Subject: [PATCH] webshare: a claimed Windows or macOS device reports a cancelled share

Patch 0065 enables the WebShare runtime feature whenever the profile claims a
platform whose Chrome ships it, so navigator.share and navigator.canShare exist
on a Linux build under a Windows or macOS persona. It left the call itself
alone, and said so: share() still goes through the platform ShareService, which
has no Linux implementation.

What that actually produces is narrower and sharper than "rejects". Measured by
reading the emitter rather than inferred: chrome/browser/webshare/BUILD.gn builds
share_service_impl only for is_win || is_chromeos || is_mac, so on Linux nothing
binds blink::mojom::ShareService, the pipe closes, and the renderer's disconnect
handler runs. That handler is NavigatorShare::ShareClientImpl::OnConnectionError,
and it rejects with

    AbortError, "Internal error: could not connect to Web Share interface."

Upstream already picks the right DOMException name there by accident of design:
ShareError::CANCELED maps to kAbortError too, in Callback a few lines above, so a
share the user dismissed on a real Mac and a Linux build with no backend are the
same name. The message is the entire difference, and the message names the Web
Share *interface* -- a string no Windows or macOS Chrome can emit, reachable with
one .catch(e => e.message). A detector does not need to understand Mojo to read
it.

So this changes the message and nothing else, for the launches 0065 created. The
condition is deliberately the same test 0065 makes, profile->ua_platform() equal
to "Windows" or "macOS", so the set of launches that take the new branch is
exactly the set that has the interface at all. With no profile, or a persona
whose Chrome ships no share backend, the upstream text is untouched. The
replacement string is taken from ErrorToString rather than copied, so this and a
genuine CANCELED cannot drift apart later.

Reporting a cancel was chosen over the two alternatives, and the reasoning is
what decides it rather than convenience. Resolving would be the lie: the page
would show a success for a share that never happened, and a user who clicked
Share would be told their link went somewhere. Withdrawing navigator.share on a
Linux host would undo 0065 and put back a missing interface the claimed device
has, which is a stronger signal than any message string. Reporting a cancel is
true in both directions at once -- nothing was shared, which is what the page is
being told -- and the cancel branch is the one every site with a share button
already handles, because people dismiss share sheets constantly. The user's
outcome is the same as tapping outside the sheet, which is a normal thing to
have happen.

This is the renderer's exception emitter, not an accessor or a wrapper, and it is
the only place the string exists. Patching the browser instead would mean
compiling a Linux ShareService in order to have it report a cancel, which is more
code in more files for the same observable, and it would leave the message wrong
on any other platform where the interface fails to bind.

What remains true afterwards, and is a residual rather than a fix: no share sheet
appears, so the rejection is prompt where a real one waits for the user. That is
inside the real distribution rather than outside it -- a user can dismiss a sheet
immediately, and Windows ShareOperation can fail before showing one -- and the
alternative is worse. A fixed delay is a signature of its own, and a randomised
one would be per-call variation, which this project does not do because two reads
of one surface in one launch must agree. Web Share also requires transient user
activation, so a page that has not been clicked gets NotAllowedError on every
platform identically and cannot reach this path at all.

Ledger: api.webshare-presence.

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webshare/navigator_share.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webshare/navigator_share.cc
@@ -6,8 +6,11 @@
 
 #include <stdint.h>
 
+#include <optional>
+#include <string>
 #include <utility>
 
+#include "base/apostate/profile.h"
 #include "base/files/safe_base_name.h"
 #include "build/build_config.h"
 #include "services/network/public/mojom/permissions_policy/permissions_policy_feature.mojom-blink.h"
@@ -173,9 +176,46 @@
 }
 
 void NavigatorShare::ShareClientImpl::OnConnectionError() {
+  // Apostate: a device claiming Windows or macOS reports the share the way
+  // that device is able to -- as the one the user dismissed.
+  //
+  // Patch 0065 enables the WebShare runtime feature whenever the profile
+  // claims a platform whose Chrome ships it, so navigator.share exists on a
+  // Linux build under a Windows or macOS persona. share() then asks the
+  // browser for blink::mojom::ShareService, which chrome/browser/webshare/
+  // BUILD.gn builds only for is_win || is_chromeos || is_mac. On Linux there
+  // is no binder for it, the pipe closes, and this runs. So this function is
+  // reached by exactly the launches 0065 created, and the condition below is
+  // deliberately the same test 0065 makes.
+  //
+  // The DOMException name upstream already produces here is the right one:
+  // ShareError::CANCELED maps to kAbortError as well, so a cancelled share on
+  // a real Mac and this are indistinguishable by name. Only the message
+  // differed -- and the message names the Web Share *interface*, a string no
+  // Windows or macOS Chrome can emit, which one .catch(e => e.message) reads.
+  // That string was the entire tell.
+  //
+  // Reporting a cancel is honest in both directions at once. Nothing was
+  // shared, which is what the page is being told, and the cancel branch is the
+  // one every site with a share button already handles, because users dismiss
+  // share sheets constantly. Resolving would have been the lie: the page would
+  // show a success it never got.
+  //
+  // The string comes from ErrorToString rather than a copy of it, so this and
+  // a genuine CANCELED cannot drift apart in a later revision.
+  bool claims_share_platform = false;
+  if (const ::base::apostate::Profile* profile =
+          ::base::apostate::Profile::Get()) {
+    if (const std::optional<std::string>& claimed = profile->ua_platform()) {
+      claims_share_platform = (*claimed == "Windows" || *claimed == "macOS");
+    }
+  }
   resolver_->Reject(MakeGarbageCollected<DOMException>(
       DOMExceptionCode::kAbortError,
-      "Internal error: could not connect to Web Share interface."));
+      claims_share_platform
+          ? ErrorToString(mojom::blink::ShareError::CANCELED)
+          : String("Internal error: could not connect to Web Share "
+                   "interface.")));
 }
 
 NavigatorShare& NavigatorShare::From(Navigator& navigator) {
